[{:db/id -1
  :adr/id "adr-2608291300-bots-return-to-murakumo-main-and-the-pool-stops-rotating"
  :adr/title "Bots return to murakumo-main, and the gateway pool stops rotating its leader"
  :adr/status "accepted"
  :adr/date "2026-08-29"
  :adr/related "adr-0072 adr-2608272400-a-provider-that-names-its-own-outage adr-2608270100 root:ADR-2608272200 root:ADR-2608271900"
  :adr/body "## Owner instruction\n\n2026-08-29: 「itonami cli の bots がまだ grok bots, hermes bots のように成熟度、安定度が高くない。原因を特定して、murakumo cloud api の murakumo main モデルで安定して動くようにして。必要であれば murakumo 側も整えて」\n\n## What was measured before changing anything\n\nLive store, turns since 2026-08-26: 207 turns, 160 failed (77%). Dominant causes:\n\n| error | n |\n|---|---|\n| :provider/fallback-failed | 106 |\n| :internal-error | 17 |\n| :provider/denied | 14 |\n| :agent/parallel-tool-limit | 10 |\n| :workspace/unsafe-path | 6 |\n\nThe fallback-failed row is the 2026-08-27/28 OpenRouter free-plan configuration failing exactly the way its own config comments predicted: the pinned free model (nemotron, then minimax) disappeared or 400'd within ~24h, and the router fallback failed on the replayed continuation. Free-tier volatility is not a defect of this app, but standing on it made the app's stability depend on it.\n\nWhy the bots had LEFT murakumo (each since fixed): the alias-guard staleness that refused healthy answers (ADR-2608270100), the 250 ms single retry that could not absorb gateway 502s (ADR-2608272400), the shipped catalog's :max-transient-retries 0 for murakumo, the workforce provider-id hardcode (bd5bc5b), and the fleet /ready lying about a dead node (root ADR-2608271900).\n\nWhat grok bots and hermes bots do differently, and why they survived the same weeks: they dereference the murakumo-main ALIAS instead of pinning a checkpoint id (ADR-2608201400: grok-bots passed a model swap untouched for exactly this reason), and hermes bots MEASURE candidate models at install time instead of trusting a name (free-model-policy.edn). The itonami bots now do both: the alias via config, the measurement via this day's probes.\n\n## The two murakumo-side defects found by measurement, 2026-08-29\n\nProbed through the public gateway api.murakumo.cloud (/v1/chat/completions, model murakumo-main):\n\n1. **gad decoded at 2.7–8 tok/s on every sample while b70 decoded at 53 tok/s.** The pool rotated its leader (b70 2-in-3, gad 1-in-3), so one resident turn in three led with an origin that cannot finish a 512-token completion inside any timeout. Rotation also split the llama.cpp prefix cache: an 18k-token resident transcript re-evaluated from zero (~83 s on gad) whenever the leader changed, and answered in 7–13 s when it did not.\n2. **The non-streaming gateway timeout ignored input size**: 30 s + 120 ms/token gave a 512-token turn 91 s while its own prompt evaluation took 83 s. Healthy resident turns were aborted by their own gateway.\n\nBoth fixed in network-awai/local-murakumo c718f89 (merge of agent/bot-stability-murakumo-pool), deployed as api.murakumo.cloud version 86f0a34a: b70 leads unconditionally with gad as failover only, and inference-timeout-ms gains the same 4 ms-per-input-character allowance streaming already had. Suites: cljs 191/982, kotoba 3, origin-proxy 4, origin-auth 6, chat_fallback_timeout 12, grok_bots_alias 2 — all green; negative control on the pool order fails by name. Verified live post-deploy: three consecutive 18k-token requests all served by b70, first 28.7 s (pp 884 tok/s, cache cold), then 12–24 s cached. West pin advanced (root f831238c).\n\n## Decision\n\n1. This instance's operator config (data/config.edn, gitignored) routes bots to provider murakumo, model murakumo-main. openrouter stays enabled as a reviewed manual fallback.\n2. The murakumo provider override sets :max-transient-retries 2 (the shipped catalog's 0 predates provider-retry ADR-2608272400 and, with no fallback entry for murakumo-main, made a single transient 502 fail the whole turn) and :model-request-timeout-seconds 420 for murakumo-main (a cache-miss large turn that fails over to gad can legitimately take ~270 s; 180 s filed that as provider/timeout).\n3. MURAKUMO_API_KEY is set at launch so provider admission's derived `authenticated` fact is true. /v1/chat/completions is today an unauthenticated public route (verified in worker source and live), so the gateway does not validate the value; the variable carries a real credential of that Worker (the /v1/messages secondary acceptance slot) so a later auth tightening does not strand this instance. The first-class mk1 signing key MURAKUMO_API_TOKEN_SECRET remains ABSENT from the kagi vault (measured 2026-08-21, re-confirmed 2026-08-29) — minting a proper chat-scope token needs owner recovery of that item.\n\n## What this does not claim\n\nThe remaining failure classes (:internal-error 17, :agent/parallel-tool-limit 10, :workspace/unsafe-path 6 over the window) are app-side and not addressed by this switch; they are the next targets once the provider floor holds. The fleet inventory defect (root ADR-2608271900, /nodes reporting load1=None for every node) is still present and untouched — the chat path does not depend on it. The .kotoba mirror kotoba/inference_timeout_core.kotoba in local-murakumo still carries the 1-arity decision and a pre-existing 45 s floor drift from the cljc; it is compile-verified but not runtime-consumed, noted rather than silently left."}]
